From 0019c2d3e4f50617283949a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 22:05:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA/HIP: release the q8_1 reuse cache before the pools are
 destroyed

The series aborts at CUDA context teardown with GGML_ASSERT(pool_size == 0).
Patch 0003's q8_1 reuse cache owns pool allocations, and it is declared ahead of
`pools` in ggml_backend_cuda_context. Members are destroyed in reverse
declaration order, so the pools go first and the cache then returns its buffers
to a destroyed pool; ggml_cuda_pool_leg's destructor catches the leftover bytes
and aborts the process.

The destructor BODY runs before any member is destroyed, so clearing the cache
there is what puts the two back in order.

No effect on anything that is measured: the cache is already cleared on entry to
every graph evaluation, and this only runs when a context is freed. llama-server
never frees one, which is why the trusted runner never saw this. llama-bench
frees one per configuration and aborted between them, so anyone reproducing the
frontier with llama-bench hit it - including a plain -p 512 -n 128 run, which is
the obvious way to check both halves of the score at once.

Verified on gfx1201: llama-bench with -ngl 99 -p 512 -n 128 completes and prints
both rows with this patch applied, and aborts with the pool assert without it.
Perplexity is unchanged at 5.2611.

Wins no score; it makes the series reproducible.
---
 ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu |   11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
index 0b3283757..30d275767 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
@@ -701,6 +701,17 @@ static std::condition_variable ggml_cuda_lock_cv;
 static std::atomic<int> ggml_cuda_lock_counter;
 
 ggml_backend_cuda_context::~ggml_backend_cuda_context() {
+    // The q8_1 reuse cache owns pool allocations, and it is declared ahead of
+    // `pools` in the context. Members are destroyed in reverse declaration order,
+    // so on teardown the pools would go first and the cache would then return its
+    // buffers to a destroyed pool - ggml_cuda_pool_leg's destructor catches that
+    // as GGML_ASSERT(pool_size == 0) and aborts the process. The destructor BODY
+    // runs before any member is destroyed, so releasing the cache here is what
+    // keeps the two in order. Only reached at context teardown; a server that
+    // never frees its context never saw it, but llama-bench frees one per
+    // configuration and aborted between them.
+    quantized_src1_cache.clear();
+
     std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(ggml_cuda_lock);
     ggml_cuda_lock_cv.wait(lock, []{ return ggml_cuda_lock_counter.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) == 0; });
 
